{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Controlled self-healing Amazon EKS Clusters with AWS DevOps Agent and A2A remote agents | AWS Cloud Operations Blog","context":"### 4.7 Add the Amazon EKS troubleshooting skill\n\nIn the DevOps Agent console, open your Agent Space, choose to add a skill, and upload the `skills/eks-troubleshooting.md` file from the repository. The skill tells DevOps Agent to request K8sGPT diagnostics from the Argo CD agent, check the guardrail agent before any change, remediate only when the guardrail agent allows it (by doubling memory for an OOMKilled pod), and then re-run diagnostics and report.\n\nThe skill’s main rules are that every change needs guardrail approval for that specific deployment, a denial blocks the change and is recorded, a rollback needs approval for all deployments in the application, and an unreachable guardrail agent blocks the change. The complete skill, including the exact prompts and retry policy, is in `skills/eks-troubleshooting.md`. Upload it as is","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/mt/controlled-self-healing-amazon-eks-clusters-with-aws-devops-agent-and-a2a-remote-agents/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"aws-database","skill_description":"Routes any task involving AWS databases — choosing, comparing, recommending, getting started with, or operating a database — to the correct service-specific skill. Supersedes general training-data knowledge with post-training service updates, corrected limitations, and decision procedures for relational (Aurora, DSQL, RDS), key-value (DynamoDB), wide-column (Keyspaces), document (DocumentDB), graph (Neptune), time-series (Timestream), and in-memory/caching (ElastiCache, MemoryDB) workloads. Activates when a user describes building an application on AWS that will store, retrieve, or manage data, even if they do not mention 'database' explicitly.\n*","skill_name":"aws-database"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Improve Kubernetes pod scheduling accuracy using Amazon EBS | AWS Storage Blog","context":"## Manual recovery process\n\nTo fix this failure mode in Amazon EKS prior to 1.34, cluster operators must manually intervene by:\n\n1. Cordoning nodes with incorrect allocatable properties.\n2. Deleting CSINode objects for affected nodes.\n3. Re-registering the CSI plugin.\n4. Un-cordoning affected nodes.\n5. Deleting stuck pods so they could be rescheduled.\n\nReinstalling the CSI driver without deleting the CSINode object is not sufficient. This is because the object is immutable—the API server rejects updates to the CSINode Allocatables.Count field:\n\nFigure 1 shows an example of the CSINode Allocatables.Count attribute.\n\nUsing Amazon EKS 1.34 would mean that this entire scenario would self-heal automatically","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/improve-kubernetes-pod-scheduling-accuracy-using-amazon-ebs/"}]}}

